# Design-Spec First Workflow (Figma-Aligned)

## Purpose
Primary goal: create and maintain `components/ids/<slug>/design-spec.mdx` (IDS) or `components/synapse/<slug>/design-spec.mdx` (Synapse) with maximum fidelity to Figma.
Implementation/code generation is optional and only done when explicitly requested by the user.

## New spec intake (wizard-first)
- For **new** design specs, prefer the intake wizard: user pastes the **base prompt** from `docs/design-spec-intake.md` or invokes **design-spec-intake-wizard** in Cursor.
- Agent asks programme, component, Figma URLs (component required; elements/states optional), Storybook yes/no — **one question per turn** — then **confirm** before Figma or file writes.
- Follow `docs/design-spec-authoring-contract.md` for paths, sections, and live verification.
- Create `components/<programme-dir>/<slug>/` and update the figma map when absent (see programme config under `config/design_systems/`).

## Source of Truth
- Always resolve the component from a design-system map file first (for example `data/component-figma-map.json` or `data/<design-system>-component-figma-map.json`).
- Use mapped `figmaUrl` + `node-id` for the component when available.
- If multiple map files exist, pick the one for the active design system before extracting spec details.
- If no map entry exists, explicitly record the manual Figma URL/node used in spec metadata.
- Figma semantic variables (`var(--...)`) are authoritative for token naming.
- If implementation tuning is required after visual review, update the design spec so regeneration preserves behavior.
- **Live Figma verification is mandatory** for any design-spec creation, update, or verification request. Do not finalize based only on existing repo content or user-provided URLs.
- Live verification must use Figma MCP tools (preferred) or Figma REST API when MCP is unavailable.
- Minimum required live checks:
  - node/frame metadata (structure + dimensions)
  - variable/token bindings for the target nodes
  - state/variant evidence from the referenced nodes or component sets

## Design-System Agnostic Requirements
- The workflow must not assume IDS/Synapse-only naming or folder structure.
- Keep spec language generic so the same contract applies to any target framework and any design system.
- Component metadata must include design system identity and source mapping used for extraction.
- Asset contracts (icons/illustrations) must be explicitly documented with stable slug-to-file mapping.

## Required Spec Sections
For each `components/ids/<slug>/design-spec.mdx` (IDS) or `components/synapse/<slug>/design-spec.mdx` (Synapse), ensure these are concrete and non-placeholder:
- `## Metadata`
- `## Layout & Measurements`
- `## Tokens`
- `## States (Light Theme)`
- `## States (Dark Theme)`
- `## Interactions`
- `## Composition & API (runtime)`
- `## Codegen Contract (Framework-Agnostic Blueprint)`
- `## Source Mapping`

## Mandatory Blueprint Content (inside Codegen Contract)
- Deterministic slot/anatomy schema (required order + optional branches).
- Supported variant/option matrix (all valid combinations).
- Per-slot style contract using semantic tokens (not only global token list).
- Behavior contract (state transitions, timers, triggers, close/open logic).
- Accessibility contract (roles, aria attributes, keyboard behavior).
- Asset resolution + bundling contract (icons/images/fonts if used).
- Fallback/error rules for unknown variants, missing tokens, missing assets.
- Validation checklist with pass/fail items suitable for automated codegen QA.

## Production-Ready Gate (must pass before marking spec ready)
- Metadata is complete and points to validated source nodes.
- Composition/API section has explicit prop/event contracts and defaults.
- Codegen Contract exists and is internally consistent with other sections.
- **States (Light Theme)** holds the full state matrix when light and dark use the same semantic `var(--...)` names; **States (Dark Theme)** may use the standard pointer boilerplate (see Spec Authoring Rules) instead of duplicating identical tables.
- When light and dark genuinely use different `var(--...)` per row, both sections must include parallel matrices.
- No unresolved placeholders like "TBD", "sample only", or ambiguous behavior language.
- Asset contracts include stable slug-to-file mapping and bundling rule (if assets are used).
- Fallback/error behavior is defined for unknown variants and missing inputs.
- Validation checklist is actionable and testable.

## Spec Authoring Rules
- Use semantic token syntax in spec: `` `var(--token-name)` ``.
- Include Light/Dark resolved values where possible.
- Never hardcode colors, spacing, typography, radius, border width, or motion values in implementation guidance; always reference CSS variables/design tokens.
- State tables must be explicit and deterministic:
  - Background
  - Border
  - Text/Icon
- **States dedupe (default for new specs):** When **States (Light Theme)** and **States (Dark Theme)** would list the same semantic `var(--...)` in every cell, document the matrix only under Light. Under Dark, use `DARK_STATES_BOILERPLATE` from `scripts/design_spec_template.py`: same tokens as Light; resolved dark values live in `components/ids-theme.css`, program theme CSS (for example `components/dap-theme.css`), and `[data-theme="dark"]` / `.ids-theme-dark`. Duplicate a full Dark table only when a dark row uses different `var(--...)` than the corresponding light row.
- **Literal exception:** If state cells use authoritative hard-coded `#hex` / `rgb()` (tokens not yet in theme), keep both tables until migrated; do not auto-dedupe (`scripts/dedupe_states_dark_theme.py` skips them).
- When both sections include tables, Light and Dark matrices must remain structurally parallel (same rows/columns).
- Separate fixed values from sample/reference values.
  - If Figma frame width is sample-only, state runtime behavior explicitly (e.g., container-driven width).

## Layout/Behavior Fidelity Rules
- Record behavior discovered during visual validation in spec (do not keep only in generated code).
- Typical examples to capture when applicable:
  - panel grouping model
  - contiguous rows / overlap spacing
  - active indicator geometry and continuity
  - header/content border continuity
  - responsive width behavior (`width: 100%`, `box-sizing: border-box`)

## Interaction Contract Rules
Document and implement interaction behavior in spec:
- Define interaction triggers for the component (click, keyboard, hover, focus, etc.).
- Define explicit state transitions for each interactive state.
- Define visual cues per state (icons, motion, outlines, indicators, cursor changes).
- If multiple interaction modes/strategies exist, document each mode and transition rules.
- Component-specific behavior takes precedence and must be declared in the component’s `design-spec.mdx` under the correct design-system folder (`components/ids/...` or `components/synapse/...`).
- Runtime default must stay interactive.
- Forced state attributes (`data-state`) are demo/testing-only and must not block runtime interaction.

## Optional Implementation / Codegen Rules
Only apply this section when the user explicitly asks for implementation or code generation.
- Framework is user-driven and not fixed: Angular, React, Vue, Lit, or other requested target.
- Parse design spec sections, do not hardcode drift-prone values.
- Treat design spec as single source of truth; generated code must be derivable from spec without hidden assumptions.
- Never hardcode colors, spacing, typography, or other styling values; always use CSS variables.
- Apply design tokens for all styling values.
- All design tokens from Figma are defined as CSS variables in `theme.css`; always use these variables instead of hardcoded values.
- Generate:
  - framework-specific component output (based on user request)
  - optional demo/sandbox output (if requested)
  - optional global-vars stylesheet output (if requested)
- Use CSS vars with parsed fallback values.
- Use layout components/utilities instead of writing custom layout CSS with Grid/Flexbox where the target framework/design system provides them.
- Avoid custom layout CSS; only add layout CSS when explicitly required and no layout component/utility exists.
- If spec defines interaction API/modes, implement those contracts in the chosen framework.
- Use `components/theme.css` for global css and refer this in generated component examples.
- When framework-specific component generated make sure there should be document notification where `theme.css` should be used in the application.

## Global Token CSS Rules
- Build global CSS vars from Figma collections:
  - `Tokens`
  - `Primitive`
  - `Density Primitive`
- Prefer collection priority to resolve duplicate codeSyntax names:
  - `Tokens` > `Primitive` > `Density Primitive`
- Produce both Light and Dark blocks.
- store it under `components` folder. 
- DO NOT create this for each component but check for any changes and update.

## Optional Drift Validation Rules
Only apply this section when user requests token drift validation.
- Compare generated CSS vars with live Figma token values.
- Report:
  - checked vars
  - mismatches
  - missing in figma map
- Handle duplicate codeSyntax values safely (set-based comparison accepted).
- Goal: mismatches should be zero before finalizing.

## Visual Validation Loop
- Design-spec validation is mandatory.
- Implementation validation is optional (run when codegen/implementation is requested).
1. Validate spec sections and token/state mappings against Figma.
2. If implementation exists, compare rendered states against Figma screenshots/frames.
3. If mismatch:
   - adjust implementation (if in scope)
   - update spec with exact corrected behavior
   - re-run generation/validation as applicable
4. Repeat until match is acceptable ("at-most design match").

## Evidence Logging Requirement
- Every finalized design spec must include live-source evidence in metadata/source-mapping:
  - file key + node ids used for validation
  - verification method (`Figma MCP` or `Figma REST API`)
  - date/time (or session context) of last live verification

## Completion Checklist
- [ ] Spec sections are concrete and Figma-aligned.
- [ ] Codegen Contract is complete and deterministic.
- [ ] Variant/option matrix is explicit and complete.
- [ ] Per-slot token mapping is explicit.
- [ ] Interaction contract is in spec.
- [ ] Accessibility and asset/bundling contracts are documented.
- [ ] Implementation/codegen executed only if explicitly requested.
- [ ] If implementation exists, generated component reflects spec behavior.
- [ ] If requested, global vars generated from required collections.
- [ ] If requested, drift checker runs with zero mismatches.
- [ ] Visual deltas (if any) are documented in spec notes.
